# Chapter Fifteen — The Grain

= Draft: dramatic conversation cut 0.1. Monday morning. Henry gives Ward the car, Ruth comes to be told first, and Henry refuses the third account with her own sentences. The fourth account is the truth. Ward arrests her. Henry stays until she is taken. The working crime engine is now on the page in full. No interaction branches yet.

## Scene 46 — Seven o'clock

= Present: Henry, alone; Ward on the telephone.

= Immediate pressure: Henry promised Ward and Nell that he would call today. He has the number in front of him and a train at ten past eight.

INT. CALDER INVESTIGATIONS - MORNING

At six fifty on Monday the card in the frosted-glass door says CLOSED, which is true. The fan is off. Two cups stand upside down on the towel where he left them Thursday. He sits at his desk in yesterday's shirt with the legal pad in front of him and the telephone beside it.

At seven he dials. Ward answers on the first transfer.

WARD (TELEPHONE)
Calder.

HENRY
You said tomorrow was a day you had.

WARD (TELEPHONE)
It's seven.

HENRY
You said it was a day.

WARD (TELEPHONE)
Go on.

HENRY
Thursday evening. I drove Ruth Ellery home from the Kenton Rooms. On Alvarado, under the freeway, she asked me if I knew what the last thing Paul said to her was. I said no. She said, "Help me." Then she said, "Not 'help me tell her.' Not 'help me with the words.' Just that. And I said no."

There is a silence on the line. Henry can hear Ward not writing.

WARD (TELEPHONE)
Thursday.

HENRY
Yes.

WARD (TELEPHONE)
Before the gallery.

HENRY
Before I knew there was a lip. On Thursday it was a sister telling me the last thing her brother said in a vestibule. On Friday you said "waiting," and I knew where it had been said from.

WARD (TELEPHONE)
And Friday night you gave me "pleased with himself."

HENRY
Yes.

WARD (TELEPHONE)
Not this.

HENRY
No.

WARD (TELEPHONE)
Why?

Henry looks at the legal pad.

HENRY
Because I wanted it to be the vestibule.

Ward lets that sit.

WARD (TELEPHONE)
That's honest. It's late. Go on.

HENRY
Last night, after the others left, she gave me a third account. She heard paper tear, went up, he was on the lip, she had his right wrist in both hands, she could hold him and not lift him, and then she could not hold him. She said his hands were white. She said nobody said anything. I asked twice.

WARD (TELEPHONE)
Nobody said anything.

HENRY
Nobody said anything.

WARD (TELEPHONE)
She can't have both.

HENRY
No.

WARD (TELEPHONE)
Where is she?

HENRY
She'll come here. She asked me to tell her first. I didn't promise. She'll come anyway.

WARD (TELEPHONE)
Then I come there. You don't say a word of it until I'm in the room.

HENRY
I'll say it to her once, in your hearing.

WARD (TELEPHONE)
You're a witness.

HENRY
I'm her one person. She said so on Saturday in your corridor, and you let her.

Ward does not answer that.

WARD (TELEPHONE)
Write it down.

HENRY
What?

WARD (TELEPHONE)
The car. Before she gets there. I want the version you had before she looks at you.

Henry pulls the pad toward him and writes, under the three questions from Thursday and the line under *two boxes*:

*Help me.*

*And I said no.*

WARD (TELEPHONE)
Half past.

HENRY
There's a train at ten past eight.

WARD (TELEPHONE)
Not for you.

The line goes dead. Henry replaces the receiver and does not turn the card.

## Scene 47 — It says closed

= Present: Henry and Ruth, in Henry's office.

= Immediate pressure: she has come to be told first. He has told Ward first. Ward is twenty minutes away.

INT. CALDER INVESTIGATIONS - MORNING

At eight minutes past seven, someone knocks directly beneath Henry's painted name.

HENRY
It's open.

RUTH
It says closed.

HENRY
I know what it says.

He opens the door. Ruth is in the coat from the police station and has not slept in it. She looks at him, then at the room. The chairs, the cabinet, the fan, the two cups on the towel.

RUTH
You didn't call.

HENRY
I called Ward.

RUTH
Before me.

HENRY
You didn't ask me to promise.

RUTH
No. I asked you to tell me first.

HENRY
Then I'm telling you first that I called Ward.

She takes the chair that does not face the window. He rests against the filing cabinet.

HENRY
Coffee?

RUTH
At this hour?

HENRY
It's the same coffee.

RUTH
Then yes.

He pours two cups and gives her one. She holds it in both hands and drinks from it, which she has not done in his office in six years. He notices. He lets it pass without pretending not to.

RUTH
He's coming here.

HENRY
Half past.

RUTH
Then say it before he gets here.

HENRY
No.

RUTH
Because you want to know whether it changes.

HENRY
Because I want him to hear that it doesn't.

Ruth looks at the legal pad on the desk. It is upside down to her, and she can read upside down; Henry has watched her do it since the old case. She reads the two lines at the bottom and does not react to them.

RUTH
I arranged the funeral. Thursday at eleven. I wrote the notice for the paper. Eleven words. It took forty minutes.

HENRY
What were they?

RUTH
Paul Ellery. His name and two dates and the word "brother." I could not find a verb I would stand behind.

HENRY
There's one.

RUTH
There are several. None of them are for the newspaper.

She drinks.

RUTH
Nell went to Marian's?

HENRY
She said tomorrow.

RUTH
She says tomorrow to Marian and then she goes in the morning. She has done that since she was fifteen. Paul told me.

HENRY
He told you a great deal.

RUTH
He told me everything he needed said back to him.

She hears it and sets the cup on the floor beside the chair, where she set the first cup, on Wednesday, when it was only a dinner.

RUTH
I'm going to keep doing that. Making them. I do it when I'm frightened. You should know that it isn't contempt.

HENRY
I know what it is.

They wait. The traffic signal outside changes and red passes across the glass and goes.

RUTH
You put your hand in the door.

HENRY
Yes.

RUTH
Ward asked you why.

HENRY
He did. I said I didn't know. I know now.

RUTH
Tell me.

HENRY
You said "Don't." You said it to Paul, and I heard it, and my hand went out. That is what I have done with you for six years. I find something out, and then I listen to what you say about it.

RUTH
And this time.

HENRY
This time I found it.

Someone comes up the stairs outside, not hurrying. The shape of a man in a brown suit stands on the other side of the frosted glass and knocks once, beneath the name, where everyone knocks.

## Scene 48 — The grain

= Present: Henry, Ruth, Ward.

= Immediate pressure: there is one account left, and it is the one nobody wants.

INT. CALDER INVESTIGATIONS - MORNING

Ward comes in and closes the door behind him. He does not take a chair. He takes the place Henry usually takes, against the filing cabinet, and Henry moves to the corner of the desk, and the room has the three of them in it and no more room.

WARD
Coffee?

HENRY
Yes.

WARD
I've had your coffee.

He looks at Ruth.

WARD
Miss Ellery. You are not under arrest. You may leave. If you stay, anything said in this room is said to me.

RUTH
I know what a room is.

WARD
Then I am here.

He looks at Henry, once, and then at the wall above Ruth's head, and stays there.

Henry does not sit.

HENRY
Sunday night you gave me an account. You had his wrist. You could hold and not lift. Then you could not hold. I asked what he said. You said nothing. There wasn't time for anything. Nobody said anything. I asked twice.

RUTH
Yes.

HENRY
Thursday, in the car, under the freeway. You asked me whether I knew what the last thing he said to you was. I said no. You said, "Help me." Then you said, "Not 'help me tell her.' Not 'help me with the words.' Just that. And I said no."

Ruth does not move.

HENRY
I thought it was the vestibule. I thought it was a brother asking his sister to carry him one more time, and the sister refusing at the foot of a stair, and I thought that was the worst of it and it was bad enough. And on Friday Ward said "waiting," and I knew where it had been said from.

RUTH
I was talking about the vestibule.

HENRY
No. Wednesday night, at the elevators, you told me what he asked you in the vestibule. He said, "Help me tell her." Your words, Wednesday. Thursday you told me it was not that. You were exact. Not "help me tell her," not "help me with the words," just "help me." So it was not the vestibule. He said it somewhere else, after, and there is one place after the vestibule and you were in it on your knees.

Ruth says nothing.

HENRY
And on Sunday you told me nobody said anything there. If he asked you on the lip and you said no, your Sunday is a lie. If nobody said anything on the lip, then your Thursday is a lie, and you invented the one sentence of his you cannot forgive yourself for. You have the wrist or you have the no. You cannot keep both, and you have been trying to keep both since Thursday.

Ruth looks at the pad on the desk.

RUTH
You wrote it down.

HENRY
He told me to. Before you looked at me.

She almost smiles.

HENRY
Second thing. Sunday you said his hands were white. You could see them. You could see color on that gallery. The button is red. It is at the height of a standing face, one pace from the bay, on the upright you walked past to reach him. On your knees at the post you could not have seen it. You saw it on the way in. Saturday you told Ward you took the pages because your name was on them. You read a name on seventeen pages by that light. You walked past one red button by it.

RUTH
I was looking at him.

HENRY
You were looking at the pages. You said so. "Long enough to know I could hold him, or I could hold—." And you stopped. I'm going to finish it, because you can't. The pages. You could hold him or you could hold the pages. They were between you and the button. You don't pick up seventeen sheets and a half with a wrist in both your hands.

RUTH
Don't.

HENRY
The whiting. Two sets of marks from the stair to the bay. His, in and not out. Yours, in and out, and narrow, and they turn back to the stair. They never turn toward the upright. You never took one step toward the bell.

RUTH
Ward said that.

HENRY
Ward said it Friday. I am saying it now.

He goes on.

HENRY
The tear. Ward read it on Saturday and said what it meant. It went along the page and then across. A page one person tears goes across. A page two people are pulling goes along, until one of them lets go. You have said in every account since Friday that you heard paper tear from the vestibule. A page tears across when one person tears it. That page tore along, and it tore along because two people had hold of it. You did not hear a tear. You were one end of it.

Ruth does not answer.

HENRY
His jacket. Ward listed it Friday and did not say what it was. Two marks of whiting on the front, the width of hands, and his hands were on the lip. Somebody's hands had been on pages that had lain on those planks, and then on his chest, hard enough to print. A man who stumbles backward through a bay does not carry two hands on his coat.

Ruth looks at her hands, and puts them back in her lap.

HENRY
And Wednesday, when you came out of the lift, there was—

He stops.

It is the one that is his. He was standing beside her coffee at a quarter past nine, and he looked at her shoe, and he decided what it was. He finds that he cannot say it to her in that room. He does not know, then or afterward, whether that is mercy or the other thing.

WARD
The shoe.

HENRY
Yes.

He has said the ones that are hers, and one that was his, and Ward has said the other.

HENRY
The old case. You told me you would rather I heard it from you than found it. You've said that to me twice since Wednesday, in other words. "I don't want you deciding what I kept from you before you know what I said." "I want him to hear it from me." This is the first time I found it.

The room is quiet. Ward has not moved from the wall.

Ruth picks up the cup from the floor and holds it and does not drink.

RUTH
He asked me to carry him one more time, and I put him down.

She hears it. She looks at the cup.

RUTH
No. That's the copy. That is what I'd write for the newspaper if they let me.

She puts the cup down.

RUTH
He went up the stair with the bundle to get away from Bell, and from me, and I went up after him. Not because I heard paper. I have said that in every version since the door because it made going up sound like something that happened to me. I went up because I had seen my cover on top of his bundle, my question and his pencil, and I wanted it back. When I got to the top he had dropped the bundle and the clip had come off the top set and he was picking them up off the planks. My cover was in his hand. He held it out to me and said, "I'm putting it all in. Even this. Help me say it so she can stand to hear it."

She stops, and starts again in the same voice.

RUTH
I said no. I said I had given him the words once and Nell had carried them since May and I was not going to write him another sentence he could say as if he had discovered it. He said it wasn't for him, it was for her. He said, "You owe her that." He was right, and he was the one who said it. I took hold of the loose set. I don't know what I meant to do with it. Not give it back. He held on. It tore. It went along, the way paper does, and then it went across. He had the corner and I had the rest, and I put both hands on him and pushed.

WARD
Both hands.

RUTH
Both hands. On his chest. I don't know whether I meant it before I did it. I meant it while I was doing it. He went backward through the bay and caught the lip, and I went down on my knees and took his wrist. The right. Both my hands. His shoes found the wall. He said, "Ruth." Then he said, "Help me," and that time he meant the other thing.

Henry does not look at the pad.

RUTH
I saw the button. I saw it was red. It was one pace and my hands were full of him. The pages were on the planks between me and it. And I thought, if I ring that bell, Bell comes, and opens the door, and Paul lives, and tonight he reads all of it to Nell in his own words, with my name in it, and the words I gave him, and the not asking again, and he does it well, because he has finally found something he can say without me, and I am the sister who—

She stops.

RUTH
I'm doing it again.

HENRY
Say it plainly.

RUTH
I thought: not with my name on them. That's all it was. It was that small. I let go with one hand and pulled the pages to me. He said, "Help me." I said no. I let go with the other hand.

Nobody speaks.

RUTH
He didn't fall far. It sounded farther than it was. Then I did the thing with the pile, and the bag, and the key, and "not to wait." And on Thursday morning I put his words face down over a bottle of floor wax, and on Friday you stood next to them and I told you they weren't yours.

Ward takes one step off the wall and stops.

WARD
Did it change?

RUTH
Yes.

WARD
Then that's the one.

He does not take out a notebook. He has not needed one all week.

WARD
Ruth Ellery. I am arresting you for the murder of Paul Ellery on Wednesday night at Tolland's. You will come with me now. You may telephone a lawyer from headquarters. You do not have to say anything else, and I would rather you didn't, because I want what you said in this room to stay the shape it is.

RUTH
It will.

She stands. Ward does not touch her. He moves to the door and opens it and waits on the landing, out of the room, in hearing.

Ruth looks at Henry.

RUTH
Now you know three things.

HENRY
Three.

RUTH
Say them. I want to hear whether you can.

HENRY
You were there. You took the pages. You held him, and you had the bell, and you chose the pages.

RUTH
That's four.

HENRY
The last two are one thing.

She nods once. It is the same nod as Friday night at the tape.

RUTH
You could have made me smaller. On my knees, not strong enough. You could have made me larger. A woman who kills her brother for a byline. You did neither.

HENRY
Neither is true.

RUTH
No.

She picks up the cup from the floor and the cup from the desk, Henry's, and starts toward the sink with them.

WARD
Leave them.

She stops. She looks at the two cups in her hands for a moment, as if they were evidence of a meeting neither of them intended to admit occurred. She puts them on the desk, side by side.

RUTH
Nell.

HENRY
I'm going to tell her badly.

RUTH
Tell her the sentence was mine. The one he said when he left her. All of it. She knows. Tell her I said it again, here, so that it's said in a room with a policeman in it.

HENRY
I will.

RUTH
And Lydia.

HENRY
There's a train.

RUTH
There was.

She goes out past Ward onto the landing. Ward goes down the stairs first, and she follows him, and Henry stands in the open door and watches her go down, the coat, the back of her head, one hand on the rail. She does not look back. He does not expect her to. At the bottom she stops and, for the first time in six years, does.

Then the street door closes.

Henry stays in the doorway until he hears the sedan start in the street, and pull out, and go. Then he goes back inside and closes the door and sits in the chair that does not face the window, because it is the one that is warm.

The two cups are on the desk, side by side. After a while he takes them to the sink and washes them and puts them upside down on the towel.

He does not look at the clock until he has finished. When he does, it is twenty past eight, and a train has left Union Station without being seen off, and the card in the door still says CLOSED, and for once it is not a ritual.
